<p>After two decades of above-average precipitation and bumper crops, limited rainfall and the effects of the Great Depression became evident in Mandan. Although the drought affected local agriculture less severely than it did the central Plains, high unemployment and low business activity were evident throughout the area. According to state records, North Dakota's average per capita income in 1933 was only $145, compared with $375 nationwide. Federal Works Progress Administration and Civilian Conservation Corps projects in the area included the World War Memorial Building (now Mandan City Hall), restoration of the Slant Indian Village earth lodges, reconstruction of the original rodeo grounds, and the addition of several buildings at Fort Abraham Lincoln State Park.</p>

