<p>During the early 1930s, the Mandan City Directory listed 16 grocery stores in Mandan, 12 of which had Main Street addresses. Most of these stores were small "mom and pop" businesses, and a few were owned by German-speaking families that attracted immigrant farmers who hadn't as yet learned to speak English. The Kist Market was at 318 West Main St. The Red Owl Store was located at 118 West Main St.</p>

<h3>1930</h3>

<p>In January, fire consumed the rear of the Lewis and Clark Hotel. In May, Fargo architect {% person "w-e-kurke" %}W. E. Kurke{% endperson %} coordinated the repair and expansion of that area of the building to three stories featuring 10 apartments and 10 additional hotel rooms at a cost of {% inflation 18000, 1930 %}.</p>

<h3>1931</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1930s/200-eielson-field-1931-airmail-fdc.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Mandan in the &quot;Dirty Thirties&quot; 1930-1939 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The area's first airmail service began on June 2, 1931. A Northwest Airways Hamilton monoplane with a 525-horsepower engine and "Arctic" tires departed at 8:33 a.m. from a temporary landing field at the McGillic farm, 2 miles west of Mandan. Of the 324 pounds of mail—approximately 15,000 individual pieces—187 pounds originated in Mandan and 137 pounds came from Bismarck. Both cities were competing to be selected as the service's single site. The general superintendent of the U.S. Airmail Service and his assistant attended.</p>

<h3>1932</h3>

<p>The first public "swimming hole" was established for community-wide use. The Mandan Lions Club initiated an effort to raise money to create a "natural swimming pool" by installing an impoundment across the Heart River adjacent to Riverside Park. Various civic service clubs participated in the fundraising effort. A significant amount of the work was completed by area volunteers and coordinated by the Chamber of Commerce with {% person "l-m-tavis" %}L. M. Tavis{% endperson %} serving as the chairman of the "General Swimming Pool Committee."</p>

<h3>1934</h3>

<p>With the completion of the first floor and indoor swimming pool of Mandan's World War Memorial Building, a four-day celebration was held in October with more than 4,000 people participating from both Mandan and Bismarck. Events included musical entertainment by the Mandan Men’s Chorus and the high school orchestra, swimming and diving contests, lifesaving demonstrations, water stunts, a carnival and a bathing suit style show. Construction on the second and third floors would continue later in the year, with the building finished in 1937.</p>

<h3>1935</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1930s/184-palace-theater-street-scene-1937.jpg" alt="Palace Theatre street frontage in 1937">
    <figcaption>Palace Theatre street front, 1937</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Mandan’s Palace Theatre at 106 Third Avenue NW underwent another remodeling project with the installation of the largest and most modern cooling and air-conditioning system found in the state. According to theatre manager {% person "frank-wetzstein" %}Frank Wetzstein{% endperson %}, the machinery, costing {% inflation 6000, 1935 %}, arrived from Minneapolis in three great vans. The system was capable of keeping the air in the theater at 70 degrees even during the hottest summer day. The central part of the system is a giant seven-foot fan weighing two tons and driven by a 10-horsepower motor. The fan forced cool, clean air into the theater, replacing the room's entire air supply every minute.</p>

<p>The North Dakota State Employment Service (now Job Service North Dakota) opened its first Mandan office. In July, high water forced the Heart River over its banks, flooding the southern part of the city, including the municipal golf course. A 3-inch rainfall followed an earlier three-day total of 3 inches, and the river rose more than 3 feet overnight. Water swept down Sixth Street NW, carrying automobiles in its path. The rapidly rising river flooded "Dogtown"—Gerard's Addition in the southeastern part of town—and the Youth Correctional Facility. It came within inches of overtopping the Riverside Park dike that protected Mandan's "Syndicate" development in the southwest corner of town. In response, the Park Board undertook projects to improve and expand Riverside Park. After demolishing the existing pavilion, it built a new clubhouse with a basement and screened veranda near the Syndicate tennis courts. The first high school football games were also played on the park's new field.</p>

<h3>1936</h3>

<p>Three taxi companies operated in Mandan: 57 Taxi, 400 Taxi, and newcomer 20th Century Taxi. The Bismarck City Commission had denied 20th Century Taxi a charter, but Mandan had no taxi-licensing ordinance at the time. Mandan High School students published their first yearbook, dubbed <em>Sapakaska</em>, which means "black and white" in the Sioux language and reflects the school's colors. Mandan recorded a high of 115°F in July 1936 at the Great Plains Field Station weather bureau. The previous winter had brought extreme cold, including a record-breaking −34°F in February. The prolonged cold broke water mains and residential lines north of Main Street, turning much of Sixth Avenue into a sheet of ice. The railroad rehired many unemployed workers to keep western tracks clear of snow and ensure that coal shipments continued to arrive. Mandan received only 6.35 inches of precipitation, compared with an annual average of almost 16 inches. It was the area's driest year on record and climaxed seven consecutive years of nearly complete crop failure.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1930s/350-1936-aug-fdr.jpg" alt="President Franklin D. Roosevelt speaking with a Morton County farm boy">
    <figcaption>“This Great Nation Will Endure,” photograph by Arthur Rothstein</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>U.S. President {% person "franklin-d-roosevelt" %}Franklin D. Roosevelt{% endperson %} visited on August 27. Roosevelt rode through town in an open car with Governor {% person "walter-welford" %}Walter Welford{% endperson %}, following a cavalcade of motorcycles and patrol cars. He toured the Great Plains Research Center to review a report on the drought's local effects, then visited Nelson Dam 15 miles north of the city. A photograph by {% person "arthur-rothstein" %}Arthur Rothstein{% endperson %} showing the president speaking with a Morton County farm boy later became famous through a publication sponsored by a Depression-era federal program.</p>

<p>A 400-foot-long pedestrian underpass was built from downtown Main Street beneath the Northern Pacific rail yard to the city's south side. Two crews, starting at opposite ends, dug the concrete-lined "viaduct" by hand. It cost {% inflation 33000, 1936 %}, despite a project bid of $28,000. Residents of the Gerard's and Syndicate additions had first requested the underpass in 1934 so children would not have to cross the tracks on their way to school. Other residents also crossed the tracks at unauthorized points for quicker access to downtown rather than walking several extra blocks to the crossings at Sixth Avenue NE or Eighth Avenue NW. The tunnel closed in 1980. A new signal was also installed at the intersection to accommodate the adjacent pedestrian crosswalk.</p>

<p>The November 1936 elections set voter-turnout records across North Dakota, including Mandan and Morton County. Mandan residents cast a record 2,823 ballots, surpassing the previous mark set in 1932. Based on the ballot count, officials claimed that a new census would show the city's population had grown to well over 6,000. {% person "william-wild-bill-langer", "William Langer" %}William Langer{% endperson %}, formerly Morton County state's attorney, came from behind to defeat incumbent Republican Governor Walter Welford and Democratic candidate {% person "john-moses" %}John Moses{% endperson %} by a margin of fewer than 2,300 votes. In Morton County, returns indicated that {% person "james-hanley-jr" %}James Hanley Jr.{% endperson %} defeated {% person "c-f-kelsch" %}C. F. Kelsch{% endperson %} by 72 votes for state's attorney. North Dakotans also voted to legalize liquor, putting the state in the "wet" column for the first time since statehood.</p>

<h3>1937</h3>

<p>Mandan's J. C. Penney store would move to a larger location on West Main Street. The store had been operating from 216 W. Main Street since 1920.</p>

<h3>1938</h3>

<p>A scarlet fever epidemic reached Mandan in February. {% person "j-a-cowan" %}Dr. J. A. Cowan{% endperson %} of the North Dakota Department of Health and local businessman {% person "george-toman" %}George Toman{% endperson %} surveyed the crisis's effects. More than 70 active cases were identified, and residents of the affected homes were placed under strict quarantine for 21 days. The mortality rate attributed to the disease commonly reached 15 percent.</p>

<p>The city also grieved after a murder-suicide took the lives of the Macklem family, reportedly following a domestic disturbance. August Sr., who was in poor health and had recently become unemployed, left a note stating that the first shot was accidental and that afterward, "I lost all sense of reason." His wife, son August Jr., daughter-in-law, and infant grandson were found shot to death at the younger Macklem's home west of his parents' residence.</p>

<p>Much of the information presented on this page is based on research conducted by {% person "diane-boit" %}Diane Boit{% endperson %} on assignment to the <em>Mandan News</em> in her weekly "Those Were the Days" columns.</p>
